# Gauss–Newton

Gauss–Newton minimizes a nonlinear least-squares objective by linearizing its
residuals and taking the full least-squares step. It uses no damping or line
search, so a good starting point and a well-conditioned Jacobian matter.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gauss_newton/struct.GaussNewton.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                   |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth sum of squared residuals, or an adapted robust loss  |
  | Evaluations | `Residual` and `Jacobian`                                     |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                                |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained                                                 |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, plus nalgebra-sparse and faer-sparse |

## When to use it

Consider Gauss–Newton when the residual model is nearly linear near your
starting point and the Jacobian has full column rank. It is a useful baseline
for understanding the effect of damping. [Madsen, Nielsen, and Tingleff
(2004)](#ref-madsen-nielsen-tingleff-2004), §3.1, describe the method.

For a less reliable start, compare
[Levenberg–Marquardt](../levenberg-marquardt/). For ill-conditioned dense
Jacobians, compare its [QR variant](../levenberg-marquardt-qr/). These are local
methods; a stationary point need not be the global minimum.

## How it works

At each iterate, Gauss–Newton forms the gradient `g = Jᵀr` and Gram matrix
`JᵀJ`. It solves `(JᵀJ) h = -g` by Cholesky and moves to `x + h` without
checking whether the objective decreased. This approximates the least-squares
Hessian by omitting terms involving second derivatives of the residuals.

Forming `JᵀJ` squares the Jacobian's condition number in exact arithmetic. A
rank-deficient Jacobian can make Cholesky fail, and a highly nonlinear model can
send the full step away from a solution.

## Example

This affine residual model has its unique zero at `[1.0, 2.0]`. Its Jacobian has
full column rank, so the full Gauss–Newton step solves it directly.

<!-- compile: solver-gauss-newton -->
```rust
use basin::{DenseMatrix, Executor, GaussNewton, Jacobian, Residual};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Fit;

impl Residual for Fit {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = Vec<f64>;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn residual(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Output, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![x[0] - 1.0, 2.0 * (x[1] - 2.0)])
    }
}

impl Jacobian for Fit {
    type Jacobian = DenseMatrix;

    fn jacobian(&self, _: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Jacobian, Self::Error> {
        Ok(DenseMatrix::from_row_slice(2, 2, &[1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 2.0]))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = GaussNewton::new().with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);
    let result = Executor::from_start(Fit, solver, vec![0.0, 0.0])
        .max_iter(100)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.param()[1] - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!(result.cost() < 1e-12);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Check rank and the starting point

A non-positive-definite Gram matrix produces `SolverFailed`. Use damping or a
rank-aware method rather than expecting pure Gauss–Newton to regularize it. A
full step can also increase the cost. Keep iteration and residual-evaluation
budgets on the executor, and inspect the termination report and returned fit.

### Choose a gradient tolerance for the residual scale

`with_absolute_gradient_tolerance` tests `‖Jᵀr‖∞`, with default `1e-8`. `None`
disables the test; zero requests exact stationarity. Multiplying every residual
by a constant scales this gradient by its square. Optional observed step and
cost-change tests combine with the gradient test using OR; a small change alone
does not establish stationarity or a good fit.

## Implementation

Gauss–Newton publishes matching parameters and cost through `PointState`. It
computes the gradient internally; the state does not expose it through
`GradientState`. Residuals, Jacobians, and model caches belong to the solver.
Fresh runs reset them, while exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain them.

For ordinary residuals, the reported cost is `0.5 * sum(r[i]^2)`. The solver
requires `Residual` and `Jacobian`, without a `CostFunction` implementation.
Wrapping the problem in
[`RobustLeastSquares`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/least_squares/struct.RobustLeastSquares.html)
selects a robust loss and residual scale. The adapter supplies a safeguarded
model, while costs and convergence use the robust objective and its gradient.
Custom Jacobians additionally need `ScaleRowsInPlace` for robust losses.

Supported dense pairs are `Vec<F>`/`DenseMatrix<F>`,
`nalgebra::DVector<F>`/`DMatrix<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`/`Array2<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`/`Mat<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The default `Vec` path needs
no BLAS/LAPACK provider.

Nalgebra-sparse and faer-sparse Jacobians are also supported. Use
`GaussNewton::default()` when selecting `f32`.

